# QGuard Messaging — Windows Installer Distribution (2026-07-03)

## What ships
NSIS setup executable built by Tauri v2 (`qguard-messaging/src-tauri/tauri.conf.json`,
`bundle.active: true`, target `nsis`, v1.0.0):

- Output: `qguard-messaging/src-tauri/target/release/bundle/nsis/QGuard Messaging_<version>_x64-setup.exe`
- Size: single-digit MB (Rust exe ~7 MB + frontend dist, LZMA-compressed) — far
  below the 50 MB budget. WebView2 is NOT bundled: `webviewInstallMode:
  downloadBootstrapper` fetches it at install time only if the device lacks it
  (Windows 11 ships it by default).
- Per-user install (`installMode: currentUser`) — no admin rights needed.
- All features included: Secure Chat, Quantum Vault, video calls (with the GPU
  freeze protection from Video_Call_Machine_Freeze_Fix), workspace lock, recovery keys.

## How to rebuild + publish a new installer (v1.1.0+, with auto-update)
ONE command does everything (build with signing, AV-lock retries, publish,
signed-update manifest):

    cd qguard-messaging
    powershell -ExecutionPolicy Bypass -File tools\release-installer.ps1

Steps per release: bump `version` in src-tauri/tauri.conf.json, run the script,
update `version`/`sizeMb` in `MESSAGING_INSTALLER` (src/app/qguard-messaging/_shared.tsx),
deploy the platform (public/downloads carries both the installer and latest.json).
Remember the stale-dist env trap: `.env.local` VITE_ values are inlined at build.

## Auto-updater (added 2026-07-03, v1.1.0)
- Tauri updater plugin, minisign-signed. Keypair in
  `qguard-messaging/src-tauri/updater-keys/` (GITIGNORED — losing it means
  installed apps can never auto-update again; BACK IT UP somewhere safe).
  Pubkey + endpoint live in tauri.conf.json `plugins.updater`.
- Endpoint: `https://api.qguard.dev/downloads/latest.json` — the platform's
  public/downloads must be deployed to that domain for installed apps to see
  updates. The manifest is generated by release-installer.ps1 with the
  installer's .sig embedded.
- In-app UX: `src/components/UpdateBanner.tsx` (mounted in App.tsx) checks once
  per launch, silent on failure; "Update now" downloads with progress, then
  "Restart now" relaunches via the process plugin. Capabilities added:
  `updater:default`, `process:default`.
- Installs from before v1.1.0 have no updater — those users must manually
  download once more; every install after that self-updates.

## TURN relay (added 2026-07-03) — calls across strict NATs/countries
Client resolves ICE servers per call (src/lib/call-ice.ts), three tiers:
1. `messaging_call_relay` single-row Supabase table (migration
   027_call_relay_config.sql — APPLY IT via the SQL editor) — lets the operator
   deploy/rotate their own coturn/Twilio/Cloudflare relay with NO reinstall;
2. build-time `VITE_TURN_URL/_USERNAME/_CREDENTIAL` env override;
3. built-in fallback: free public Open Relay (openrelay.metered.ca) so
   cross-NAT calls work out of the box. Fine for testing; run your own relay
   for production scale. Relays only ever see ciphertext (E2EE media).

## Where users download it
`public/downloads/QGuard-Messaging-Setup.exe` is served statically at
`/downloads/QGuard-Messaging-Setup.exe`. The shared `DownloadButton`
(src/app/qguard-messaging/_shared.tsx) renders "Download for Windows" with the
HTML `download` attribute in the hero of:
- /qguard-messaging
- /qguard-messaging/secure-chat
- /qguard-messaging/quantum-vault

## Build reliability — os error 32 root cause (fixed 2026-07-03)
Release builds repeatedly failed with `failed to build archive ... os error 32`
(temp archive locked). Root cause: the repo lives under `C:\Users\HP\Music` — a
Windows profile LIBRARY folder that Search indexing/AV watch aggressively, and
they lock freshly written .rlib/.lib files mid-build. release-installer.ps1 now
sets `CARGO_TARGET_DIR=%LOCALAPPDATA%\qguard-messaging-target` (not indexed),
which removes the race entirely; the retry loop remains as belt-and-braces.
For manual `cargo`/`npx tauri build` runs, set the same env var (or move the
repo out of Music) if os error 32 appears.

## Known limitation — code signing
The installer is NOT code-signed (no certificate configured). Windows
SmartScreen will show "Windows protected your PC" on first runs; users click
"More info → Run anyway". For public distribution buy an OV/EV code-signing
certificate and set `bundle.windows.certificateThumbprint` (+ signtool in PATH),
or sign via Azure Trusted Signing. Until then, publish the SHA-256 of each
installer next to the download for integrity verification.
